WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.090
<v Maya>Welcome to part four.

2
00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:06.090
<v Maya>We've finished the first cardio lecture, and now we move to the second one.

3
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:14.730
<v Maya>Part four covers intensity, the levels from beginner to super advanced, and the six factors a trainer considers before prescribing cardio.

4
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.610
<v Dev>Last lecture ended with workload, right?

5
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:18.500
<v Maya>Yes.

6
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.680
<v Maya>Workload is the amount of work done, set by speed, distance and time.

7
00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:24.390
<v Maya>Today starts with intensity.

8
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.370
<v Maya>Dev, if workload is the amount of work done, what is intensity?

9
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:32.460
<v Dev>The amount of work done with respect to time, distance and speed?

10
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:35.060
<v Maya>That's actually the definition of workload.

11
00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:39.750
<v Maya>Intensity is the level of effort applied while doing that work, the difficulty level.

12
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.590
<v Maya>Here's the exam analogy.

13
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.420
<v Maya>Thirty students sit an exam with thirty multiple choice questions.

14
00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.570
<v Maya>Solving thirty questions is the workload, and it's the same for everyone.

15
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.970
<v Maya>But a student who prepared well puts in low effort, so the intensity is low.

16
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:58.260
<v Maya>A student who didn't prepare puts in high effort, so the intensity is high.

17
00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:00.670
<v Maya>Same workload, different intensity.

18
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.980
<v Dev>So how did we measure intensity in weight training?

19
00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.610
<v Maya>With the RM method.

20
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.320
<v Maya>RM is the most repetitions you can do before failure.

21
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.080
<v Maya>The lower the RM, the higher the intensity.

22
00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:17.090
<v Maya>Cardio needs its own deciding number, and it's heart rate during the cardio activity.

23
00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.040
<v Maya>The higher the heart rate during cardio, the greater the intensity.

24
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.500
<v Dev>Any heart rate?

25
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.390
<v Dev>Or something specific?

26
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.480
<v Maya>Specific.

27
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:32.120
<v Maya>Intensity is the heart rate during cardio, expressed as a percentage of the person's own maximum heart rate.

28
00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:34.640
<v Maya>Now meet our case study client, Sumit.

29
00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.910
<v Maya>He's a new client, twenty years old.

30
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.360
<v Maya>His intensity scale runs vertically.

31
00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:44.440
<v Maya>At the bottom is his resting heart rate, the heart rate from which his day starts, no effort.

32
00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.290
<v Maya>At the top is the maximum heart rate, one hundred percent effort.

33
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.020
<v Dev>What's his maximum?

34
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.520
<v Maya>Two hundred and twenty minus twenty.

35
00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.310
<v Maya>Two hundred beats per minute.

36
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.240
<v Maya>And there's a bottle analogy.

37
00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:00.300
<v Maya>A one litre bottle filled to the brim overflows if you add more water.

38
00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.510
<v Maya>In the same way, heart rate can't go beyond maximum.

39
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:10.280
<v Maya>And if you drink five hundred millilitres from the full bottle, the bottle is at fifty percent, which helps with percentages.

40
00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.200
<v Dev>Show me the calculation.

41
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:18.350
<v Maya>Intensity percent is heart rate during cardio, divided by maximum heart rate, times one hundred.

42
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.130
<v Maya>Sumit is on a treadmill.

43
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.070
<v Maya>His heart rate is one hundred.

44
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.780
<v Maya>One hundred out of two hundred is fifty percent.

45
00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:28.200
<v Maya>After five minutes it's one eighty, which is ninety percent.

46
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.950
<v Maya>Later he slows down to one sixty, which is eighty percent.

47
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.790
<v Dev>So a heart rate of one hundred is always fifty percent?

48
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.870
<v Maya>No, and this is the key warning.

49
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.700
<v Maya>Don't copy these numbers.

50
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:43.570
<v Maya>The fifty percent came from Sumit's maximum of two hundred.

51
00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.400
<v Maya>For any client, first find that client's own maximum, then take the percentage of that.

52
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:52.870
<v Maya>Two hundred was used only because a twenty year old gives easy maths.

53
00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.610
<v Dev>Quick note on the scale.

54
00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:03.840
<v Dev>The lecture writes resting heart rate as zero percent effort, but the percentages are of maximum, so eighty is really forty percent of two hundred.

55
00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:05.410
<v Dev>It's a schematic.

56
00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:06.520
<v Maya>Good point.

57
00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:10.160
<v Maya>The scale is a schematic, with resting as the no effort end.

58
00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.400
<v Dev>Recap?

59
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.770
<v Maya>Intensity is the level of effort.

60
00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:20.950
<v Maya>In cardio it's heart rate during the activity as a percentage of maximum heart rate, and higher heart rate means higher intensity.

61
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.430
<v Maya>In weight training there was a chart linking RM ranges to training levels.

62
00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.960
<v Maya>Cardio uses the same idea with percentages of maximum heart rate.

63
00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.500
<v Maya>Low intensity is fifty to sixty percent, for beginners.

64
00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:36.680
<v Maya>Moderate is sixty to seventy percent, intermediate.

65
00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:39.550
<v Maya>High is seventy to eighty five percent, advanced.

66
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.210
<v Maya>And very high is above eighty five percent, super advanced.

67
00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:47.760
<v Dev>So the high band is seventy to eighty five, not eighty to eighty five.

68
00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.710
<v Maya>Exactly, a common slip.

69
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.290
<v Maya>One small difference.

70
00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:59.710
<v Maya>The lecture says above eighty five for very high, but the slide writes greater than or equal to eighty five, so eighty five appears in both rows.

71
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.520
<v Maya>Check your course material.

72
00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:03.530
<v Dev>Who counts as a beginner?

73
00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:07.540
<v Maya>Clients with less than about two to three months of cardio experience.

74
00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.370
<v Maya>And restarting clients are also beginners.

75
00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:19.900
<v Maya>Someone who did cardio before but took a gap, for personal reasons, a health condition or rehab, can't be started at moderate or high because of old experience.

76
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.950
<v Maya>They go step by step again.

77
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.770
<v Dev>And when does a beginner become an intermediate?

78
00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.460
<v Dev>After three months?

79
00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:28.480
<v Maya>Not because the calendar says so.

80
00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:37.550
<v Maya>Imagine a client who came about three times in three months, checked social media during sessions, ignored instructions on nutrition, rest and recovery.

81
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.810
<v Maya>That client hasn't earned the next level.

82
00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:46.160
<v Maya>A fitness test is done on the first day and again after the beginner phase, for all five components of fitness.

83
00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:51.760
<v Maya>If the numbers show significant improvement, that's progress tracking, and that's how a client is promoted.

84
00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:56.850
<v Maya>Then intensity moves to sixty to seventy percent and the client is an intermediate.

85
00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.810
<v Dev>Are there rough time guides?

86
00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.430
<v Maya>Only as a rough guide.

87
00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.240
<v Maya>Beginner, under three months.

88
00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:05.030
<v Maya>Intermediate, more than three months and under a year.

89
00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:09.940
<v Maya>Advanced, after a year of consistent performance, seventy to eighty five percent.

90
00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:19.370
<v Maya>Super advanced, can go above eighty five percent easily, smoothly and safely, and come back without fatigue and without risk, like athletes and sports people.

91
00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:25.060
<v Maya>The slides give no durations, and the E-book has its own phases, so check your course material.

92
00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.870
<v Dev>Is there anything above super advanced?

93
00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.110
<v Maya>Not officially.

94
00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:40.110
<v Maya>If there were, it would be elite, people like Usain Bolt and Michael Phelps, who go very close to maximum and return safely, because their circulatory system works extraordinarily well.

95
00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.440
<v Maya>It's not everybody's cup of tea.

96
00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:46.270
<v Dev>What if a client says, I can go to two hundred and come back?

97
00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.050
<v Maya>Never take that risk, at least under your guidance.

98
00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:52.120
<v Maya>Some clients say it out of ego, even beginners.

99
00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.880
<v Maya>They might reach it and not come back from the treadmill.

100
00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:59.340
<v Maya>The cardiorespiratory system has to be made ready step by step.

101
00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:03.330
<v Dev>Does intensity change daily, if Sumit isn't feeling well?

102
00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:04.170
<v Maya>No.

103
00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:09.800
<v Maya>It's set from his current training level, and then a fitness test shows how he performs at that intensity.

104
00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:13.400
<v Maya>Consistent performance, not one day, tells whether the level works.

105
00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:15.270
<v Maya>Why do we say prescribing?

106
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:19.700
<v Maya>Because like a doctor prescribes a medicine, a trainer prescribes an intensity.

107
00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:24.310
<v Maya>And name and age aren't enough, which is why there's an initial client interview.

108
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:25.930
<v Maya>There are six factors.

109
00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.850
<v Maya>Think of them as a checklist.

110
00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.400
<v Dev>Go through them.

111
00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:31.610
<v Maya>Factor one, training experience.

112
00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.940
<v Maya>Has the person ever done cardio?

113
00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:43.930
<v Maya>If yes, which type, weight bearing impact based or the other categories, how many sessions a week, how long, and preferences like morning or evening, indoor or outdoor.

114
00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:44.970
<v Dev>Two?

115
00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.000
<v Maya>Lifestyle, a big umbrella.

116
00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:48.810
<v Maya>Food habits and nutrition.

117
00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:56.290
<v Maya>Daily routine and the nature of the job, a corporate employee on a laptop versus an architect climbing stairs on sites.

118
00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.540
<v Maya>Habits, like smoking and drinking.

119
00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:04.290
<v Maya>Sleep, both quality and quantity, seven to eight good hours versus three to four random hours.

120
00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.550
<v Maya>And stress.

121
00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:06.660
<v Dev>Three?

122
00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:08.170
<v Maya>Medical background.

123
00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.060
<v Maya>Heart, joint or neurological conditions.

124
00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:16.480
<v Maya>If the trainer is unaware and gives high intensity, the client could suffer a severe condition.

125
00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.170
<v Maya>Awareness is crucial.

126
00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:19.590
<v Dev>Four is age.

127
00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:23.820
<v Dev>Isn't that because kids and older people get different intensities?

128
00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:31.950
<v Maya>That's stereotyping, the assumption that something popular must be true, such as a sixty year old must be a beginner and a twenty year old can do more.

129
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:43.630
<v Maya>Imagine a twenty year old with no experience, junk food, poor sleep, versus a sixty year old who hasn't missed a session in thirty or forty years, with clean nutrition and a clear medical history.

130
00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.910
<v Maya>Going by age alone would be unfair.

131
00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:54.080
<v Maya>The real reason age is needed is that without age you can't calculate maximum heart rate, and without it you can't determine intensity.

132
00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:55.260
<v Dev>Five?

133
00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:56.810
<v Maya>Resting heart rate.

134
00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:04.990
<v Maya>A quantifiable number and an indicator of cardiorespiratory fitness, so it shows current fitness level on the day you meet the client.

135
00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.700
<v Maya>Sixty to eighty is normal, lower is better.

136
00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:09.010
<v Maya>And six, goal.

137
00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:20.710
<v Maya>A specific goal, like HYROX, a marathon or climbing Everest, or a basic goal, general physical preparedness, GPP, meaning basic fitness for day to day tasks and emergencies.

138
00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:22.590
<v Dev>Is the list a ranking?

139
00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:23.430
<v Maya>No.

140
00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.390
<v Maya>All six are equally important and seen together.

141
00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:33.790
<v Maya>The slides use slightly different names, age, medical background, past training history, current fitness level, lifestyle and goal.

142
00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:34.700
<v Maya>Same six.

143
00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:35.850
<v Maya>Now Sumit.

144
00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:37.620
<v Maya>Training experience, nil.

145
00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:42.740
<v Maya>Lifestyle, average for a twenty year old student, junk food, no physical activity.

146
00:08:42.790 --> 00:08:44.610
<v Maya>Medical background, clear.

147
00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:47.200
<v Maya>Age, twenty, so maximum two hundred.

148
00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.020
<v Maya>Resting heart rate, eighty.

149
00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.610
<v Maya>Goal, GPP.

150
00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:54.500
<v Maya>The decision, he starts as a beginner, fifty to sixty percent.

151
00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:56.830
<v Dev>And what does he do with a percentage?

152
00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.870
<v Dev>He can't carry a calculator on the treadmill.

153
00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:01.010
<v Maya>Exactly.

154
00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:03.950
<v Maya>The percentages are background science for the trainer.

155
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:08.310
<v Maya>The client needs a number that's easy to notice during cardio, a heart rate.

156
00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:14.590
<v Maya>So the percentage is converted into a target heart rate, THR, which the client follows on a device.

157
00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:16.090
<v Maya>That's part five.

158
00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:17.290
<v Maya>Pause and think.

159
00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.650
<v Maya>Why do we need the client's age at all?

160
00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:25.290
<v Dev>Because without age there's no maximum heart rate, and without that there's no intensity.

161
00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:26.430
<v Maya>Exactly.

162
00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:28.490
<v Maya>Here are the five things to remember.

163
00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:32.800
<v Maya>One, workload is the amount of work, intensity is the level of effort.

164
00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:38.520
<v Maya>Two, cardio intensity is heart rate during activity as a percentage of own maximum.

165
00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:45.370
<v Maya>Three, low fifty to sixty, moderate sixty to seventy, high seventy to eighty five, very high above eighty five.

166
00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:49.370
<v Maya>Four, promotion depends on fitness test results, not the calendar.

167
00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:56.620
<v Maya>Five, six equal factors, training experience, lifestyle, medical background, age, resting heart rate and goal.

168
00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:59.390
<v Dev>Next, calculating the target heart rate.

169
00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:00.690
<v Maya>See you in part five.

